      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the exponent of 3 in the prime factorization of \\(24!\\), we need to determine how many times 3 appears as a factor in the numbers from 1 to 24. This is done by counting the multiples of 3, 9, 27 (since higher powers of 3 will exceed 24), and so on.\n\nFirst, count the multiples of 3:\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{24}{3} \\right\\rfloor = 8\n\\]\nSo, there are 8 multiples of 3 in the range from 1 to 24.\n\nNext, count the multiples of 9:\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{24}{9} \\right\\rfloor = 2\n\\]\nSo, there are 2 multiples of 9 in the range from 1 to 24.\n\nThen, count the multiples of 27:\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{24}{27} \\right\\rfloor = 0\n\\]\nSo, there are 0 multiples of 27 in the range from 1 to 24.\n\nSince higher powers of 3 (like 81, 243, etc.) are greater than 24, we do not need to consider them. Adding these counts together, we get the total exponent of 3 in the prime factorization of \\(24!\\):\n\\[\n8 + 2 + 0 = 10\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the exponent of 3 in the prime factorization of \\(24!\\) is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).",
