      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the exponent of 3 in the prime factorization of \\(24!\\), we need to determine how many times the prime number 3 appears as a factor in the product of all integers from 1 to 24.\n\nFirst, we note that \\(3\\) is a factor of \\(3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18\\), and \\(21\\). The number 24 is a multiple of 3, so \\(3\\) is a factor of \\(24\\). The number 21 is also a multiple of 3, so \\(3\\) is a factor of \\(21\\).\n\nWe can count the multiples of 3 up to 24:\n\\[3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 21, 24\\]\nThere are 8 multiples of 3 in this range.\n\nNext, we count the multiples of \\(3^2 = 9\\) up to 24:\n\\[9, 18\\]\nThere are 2 multiples of 9 in this range.\n\nThen, we count the multiples of \\(3^3 = 27\\). Since 27 is greater than 24, there are no multiples of 27 up to 24.\n\nAdding these counts together, we get the total number of factors of 3 in \\(24!\\):\n\\[8 + 2 = 10\\]\n\nThus, the exponent of 3 in the prime factorization of \\(24!\\) is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).",
